OT MOOR\nThough the waters of the moor are not deep, they are reputed to have healing powers. This is where the Lady of the Lake currently dwells. Her wisdom and power rivals my own.	奧特摩爾\n沼澤之水雖不深，卻據傳具有療癒之力。湖中女神此刻便棲居於此，她的智慧與力量，堪與我匹敵。
AVEBURY\nOn this site stands the largest of the stone circles, the remains of an ancient place of worship. It is surrounded by a bank and ditch with a diameter of 1400 ft. The circle is formed of almost 100 megaliths, some weighing up to 60 tons.	埃夫伯里\n此地矗立著現存最大的環狀巨石陣，是一處古代祭祀場所的遺跡。四周環繞著一道堤岸與壕溝，直徑達一千四百呎。整座石環由近百塊巨石構成，有些重達六十噸。
MOTTISTONE\nUpon the Isle of Wight, Druid priests are reputed to have sacrificed white bulls. In this place are two great stones: an upright stone representing their God and a horizontal stone representing their Goddess. Mottistone means "the speaker's stone".	莫蒂斯通\n在懷特島上，德魯伊祭司據傳曾在此獻祭白牛。此地立有兩塊巨石：一塊豎立，代表他們的男神；一塊橫臥，代表他們的女神。「莫蒂斯通」意即「言說者之石」。
BATH\nOver 800 years before the birth of Christ, Prince Bladud, son of a king, contracted the dread disease of leprosy. Unable to remain in human company, he turned to tending swine, but they, too, caught the disease. One day the swine ran wild and plunged into the dark, foul-smelling waters of a bog. When Bladud pulled them out, he discovered that the waters had healed both the animals and himself. The healing waters of Bath are famous to this day.	巴斯\n在基督誕生前八百多年，一位國王之子——布拉德王子——染上了痲瘋這等可怕的惡疾。他無法再與世人共處，只得去放豬，豬群卻也染上了病。一日，豬群狂奔，一頭栽進一處幽暗惡臭的泥沼水塘。布拉德把牠們拉出來時，發現這水竟同時治好了牲畜與他自己。巴斯的療癒之水，至今仍負盛名。
KEYNSHAM\nA 6th century prince of Wales named Braglan had a daughter named Keyne. She came to a town wherein the lord gave her a piece of land that she might preach Christianity, but the land was heavily infested with large, venomous snakes. She at once turned the snakes into stone. She is remembered as St. Keyne and the town is named after her.	凱恩沙姆\n六世紀時，威爾斯有位名叫布拉格蘭的親王，育有一女，名喚基恩。她來到一座城鎮，鎮上的領主賜她一塊地，讓她得以傳揚基督信仰，怎料那地毒蛇為患，遍地都是巨大的毒蛇。她當場便將這些毒蛇化為石頭。後人尊她為聖基恩，這座城鎮也以她為名。
WINCHESTER\nI have heard tell that St. Swithun, the bishop of Winchester, is a performer of miracles. One story has come to my ears that he saw a poor woman on her way to a market with a basket of eggs. She bumped into a monk and all her eggs were broken. St. Swithun took pity upon her and made the eggs whole again. Or so they say.	溫徹斯特\n我聽聞溫徹斯特的主教聖斯威辛，是位能行神蹟之人。有個故事傳到我耳中：他見一名窮苦婦人提著一籃雞蛋要上市集，途中撞上一名僧侶，滿籃雞蛋盡數摔破。聖斯威辛見她可憐，便讓那些蛋一一恢復原狀。至少，傳說是如此。
SOUTHAMPTON\nThe legend goes that Sir Bevis, a hero of long ago, killed the evil giant Ascapart here. This is also the port from which one would take ship to sail to all parts of the known world.	南安普頓港\n傳說古時的英雄貝維斯爵士，便是在此地殺死了邪惡的巨人阿斯卡帕特。這裡也是啟航前往已知世界各地的港口。
CAMELOT\nYou are here, m'lord, as you surely know.	肯萊特\n你此刻便身在此處，吾王，這點你自然知曉。
GLASTONBURY TOR\nThis hill has been revered for countless centuries. To the Celts, it was Ynys-Witrin, the Island of Glass, so-called because the lands around it were often flooded. It is also said that Joseph of Arimathea, who supervised the burial of Jesus, brought the Grail here and that where he stuck his staff into the ground, a Holy Thorn tree grew. Many religious orders have since come and gone and left their ruins behind. To reach the tor now, one must travel through the Forest Perilous.	格列斯登伯利山\n這座山丘歷經無數世紀，備受尊崇。在凱爾特人口中，它喚作「Ynys-Witrin」，意即玻璃島，因四周土地常年遭水淹沒而得名。傳說監督耶穌安葬的亞利馬太的約瑟，曾將聖杯帶到此地；他將手杖插入地面之處，長出了一株聖荊棘樹。此後歷經無數教派興衰，只留下斷垣殘壁。如今要抵達此山，必須穿越派瑞樂斯森林。
SHAPWICK\nSt. Indractus, along with his sister, Drusa and other pilgrims, were passing through here on their way back to Ireland from Rome. They were carrying bags of seed corn to give to the poor. Saxon outlaws murdered them, thinking their bags were filled with gold. The crime was discovered when a beam of light shone for 3 days and 3 nights upon the burial pit. Confronted with their deed, the Saxons went mad and destroyed one another.	夏普威克\n聖印德拉克圖斯與他的妹妹杜路莎，以及其他朝聖者，當年從羅馬返回愛爾蘭途中，行經此地。他們身上背著要分送給窮人的穀種。撒克遜盜匪誤以為袋中裝的是黃金，將他們盡數殺害。後來一道光芒連續三天三夜照耀在他們的埋屍坑上，罪行方才敗露。撒克遜人得知自己所為之後，發了瘋似地自相殘殺。
HALSTOCK\nA pious woman, St. Juthware, would open her house to pilgrims on their way to a nearby shrine. This so enraged her brother that he took up his sword and cut off her head. The story says that Juthware picked up her own head and carried it to the local altar before she finally died.	哈爾斯托克\n虔誠的聖裘斯薇兒，總會敞開家門，接待前往附近神龕朝聖的信眾。此事激怒了她的兄長，他拔劍砍下了她的頭顱。傳說裘斯薇兒撿起自己的頭顱，一路帶到當地的祭壇，才終於氣絕身亡。
BRAUNTON\nMany miracles dealing with animals are connected to St. Brannoc, who reportedly sailed from Wales in a stone coffin. A sow and piglets showed him where to establish his chapel. He used wild deer to pull his plough and taught the natives how to till soil. He brought back to life a cow which had been stolen from him, killed and dismembered, but at his call it reassembled itself and returned to give him milk for the rest of its life.	布勞頓\n許多與動物有關的神蹟，都與聖布拉諾克有關，據說他乘著一具石棺從威爾斯航行而來。一頭母豬與豬崽，為他指出建立教堂之處。他以野鹿拉犁耕田，並教導當地人耕作之法。他有頭母牛被人偷走、宰殺分屍，他一聲呼喚，那頭牛竟重新聚合、復活歸來，此後終其一生都為他產乳。
HARTLAND POINT\nThe original name of this place was the Headland of Hercules, from a legend that Hercules did battle there with British giants. Another legend says that St. Nectan, a Welsh hermit, had his head cut off by robbers, but he carried his head to his well and dropped it in. Foxgloves grew wherever his blood fell.	哈特蘭角\n此地原名「海克力斯海角」，傳說海克力斯曾在此與不列顛的巨人交戰。另有一則傳說：威爾斯隱士聖尼克坦，遭盜匪砍下頭顱，他卻將自己的頭顱帶到井邊，投入井中。他的血滴落之處，此後都長出了毛地黃。
EXETER\nJust outside this city is a well and a chapel dedicated to St. Sidwell. Sidwell was a girl who had inherited some land that her stepmother wanted. The stepmother hired two men to kill the girl as she prayed in a field. They cut off her head with scythes. The spring gushes forth from the place where her head fell.	艾克塞特\n這座城市外緣有一口井與一座小教堂，都是為了紀念聖西德威爾而建。西德威爾是名少女，繼承了一塊她繼母覬覦已久的土地。繼母雇了兩名男子，趁她在田間祈禱時將她殺害，用長柄鐮刀砍下她的頭顱。她頭顱落地之處，如今仍有泉水湧出。
TOTNES\nLong ago a Trojan warrior named Brutus arrived and named this place Totnes. The story goes that he said: 'Here I am and here I rest, and this town shall be called Totnes.' He subdued two giants, Gog and Magog, and made them stand guard at his palace. Brutus is considered the legendary founder of London.	托特尼斯\n古時有位名叫布魯特斯的特洛伊勇士來到此地，將此處命名為托特尼斯。傳說他曾言：『我在此地落腳，在此地安歇，這座城鎮便叫做托特尼斯。』他降服了歌革與瑪各兩名巨人，命他們在自己的宮殿前守衛。布魯特斯被尊為倫敦的傳說創建者。
